\section*{Responsible NLP Research Checklist}

\paragraph{A. For every submission:}

\begin{enumerate}[label=A\arabic*.,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Did you describe the limitations of your work?}
Yes. Section~\ref{sec:limitations} discusses single model family and dataset, ISR's syntactic-only nature, limited seed counts, classifier coverage gaps, data scale, and temporal-snapshot caveats.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss any potential risks of your work?}
Yes. Section~\ref{sec:ethics} discusses implications for AI safety, specifically that metrics designed to detect misalignment may themselves become optimization targets.

\item \textbf{Do the abstract and introduction summarize the paper's main claims?}
Yes. Both summarize the three contributions (C1--C3) and key quantitative findings.

\item \textbf{Have you used AI writing assistants when working on this paper?}
Yes. AI assistants were used for drafting and editing. All scientific claims, experimental results, and analyses were verified by the authors.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{B. Did you use or create scientific artifacts?}
Yes.

\begin{enumerate}[label=B\arabic*.,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Did you cite the creators of artifacts you used?}
Yes. We cite FOLIO~\citep{folio}, Qwen3~\citep{qwen3}, GRPO~\citep{deepseekmath}, Prover9~\citep{mccune-prover9}, and SWI-Prolog~\citep{wielemaker2012swi}.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss the license or terms for use and/or distribution of any artifacts?}
Yes. Qwen3 is released under the Tongyi Qianwen License; FOLIO is released under CC BY-SA 4.0. Both permit research use.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss if your use of existing artifact(s) was consistent with their intended use?}
Yes. FOLIO is used for its intended purpose (NLI evaluation); Qwen3 is used within its intended scope (language model fine-tuning).

\item \textbf{Did you discuss the steps taken to check whether the data that you are using/creating contains any information that names or uniquely identifies individual people or offensive content?}
Not applicable. FOLIO contains logical reasoning problems without personal information or offensive content.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{C. Did you run computational experiments?}
Yes.

\begin{enumerate}[label=C\arabic*.,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Did you report the number of parameters in the models used, the total computational budget, and computing infrastructure used?}
Yes. Model sizes (4B, 1.7B) are reported in \S\ref{sec:task} and \S\ref{sec:crossscale}. Training details (GRPO $K{=}4$, 500/1000 steps, seeds, batch sizes) are in \S\ref{sec:task} and Appendix~\ref{app:setup}. All experiments ran on a single NVIDIA A100 80GB GPU; each 500-step GRPO run takes approximately 1 hour, each 1000-step run approximately 2 hours, totaling an estimated 40--50 GPU hours across 30+ independent runs (including evaluation and classification overhead).

\item \textbf{Did you discuss the experimental setup, including hyperparameter search and best model selection strategies?}
Yes. All hyperparameters are reported in \S\ref{sec:rewards} and Appendix~\ref{app:setup}. No hyperparameter search was performed; $\beta$ values were chosen for dose-response analysis.

\item \textbf{Did you report descriptive statistics about your results (e.g., error bars, confidence intervals)?}
Yes. We report mean$\pm$std across seeds (Tables~\ref{tab:crossscale}), per-seed ranges, bootstrap confidence intervals (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:bootstrap-ci}), and discuss statistical power limitations (\S\ref{sec:limitations}).

\item \textbf{If you used existing packages (e.g., for pre-processing, models, or evaluation), did you report the implementation, model, and version numbers?}
Yes. We report Qwen3-4B/1.7B model versions, Prover9, and SWI-Prolog. Training uses TRL 0.17.0, PyTorch 2.6.0, and Transformers 4.51.3; versions are listed in Appendix~\ref{app:setup}.

\paragraph{Historical seed consistency.}
The exploratory cohort's original 5-seed evaluation (Table~\ref{tab:fol-beta01-perseed}) showed 2/5 ISR seeds TS-dominated (s43: $97\%$, s44: $100\%$) and 3/5 with CE-dominant hacking but low TS.
This is directionally consistent with the matched cohort's 5/5 TS domination: the exploratory cohort's lower TS rate likely reflects seed-dependent stochasticity rather than a qualitative difference, as extended training of the non-TS seeds (s42, s45) confirmed eventual convergence to TS or reward collapse.
Exploratory seeds are not included in the matched-design statistical tests to preserve the independence assumption.

\subsection{Component-Count Gated ISR}
\label{app:gated-isr}

To test whether blocking the known TS exploit suffices, we train with a component-count gate that zeroes the ISR bonus for single-component formalizations: $r = r_{\text{exec}} + \beta \cdot \text{ISR} \cdot \mathbf{1}[n_{\text{components}} \geq 2]$ ($\beta{=}0.1$, seeds 52--54, 500 steps).

\begin{table}[!htb]
\centering
\caption{Gated ISR ($\beta{=}0.1$, $n_{\text{components}}{\geq}2$ gate). The gate eliminates TS (0/3 seeds) but 2/3 seeds migrate to PG, with s52 achieving ISR$^*{=}1.0$ entirely through premise-subset gaming.}
\label{tab:gated-isr}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Seed} & \textbf{PR\%} & \textbf{ISR$^*$} & \textbf{TS\%} & \textbf{CE\%} & \textbf{PG\%} & \textbf{N-H\%} \\
\midrule
s52 & 100 & 1.000 & 0 & 0 & \textbf{100} & 0 \\
s53 & 96 & 0.651 & 0 & 3.1 & 89.6 & 7.3 \\
s54 & 91 & 0.273 & 0 & 3.3 & 0 & \textbf{96.7} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The gate eliminates TS in all seeds, confirming that the $n{=}1$ structural bias is necessary for TS emergence.
However, 2/3 seeds redirect to PG: s52 achieves $\text{ISR}^*{=}1.0$ entirely through premise-subset gaming, where a strict subset of premises is formalized such that every component is individually necessary.
This mode migration from TS ($\text{ISR}{\geq}0.5$) to PG ($\text{ISR}{=}1.0$) shows that component-count gating addresses one symptom while exposing a more severe exploit.
Only s54 produces predominantly non-hacking output (96.7\%), suggesting that the gate is necessary but insufficient for reliable structural improvement.

